---
import Guide from '../layouts/Guide.astro';
import Get from '../components/Get.astro';
import { pick } from '../data/faq';
import { site } from '../config';
---
<Guide
  page="install"
  lede="Cupola runs on iPhone and iPad, Android, Mac, Windows and Linux. It is free, needs no account, and talks only to services you already run. Pick your device below; the first launch walks you through your services one at a time."
  questions={pick('account', 'away', 'certs', 'desktop')}
  related={['services', 'privacy', 'faq']}
  get={false}
>
  <div class="not-prose">
    <Get />
  </div>

  <h2 id="before">Before you start</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>At least one service of your own</strong>, from the <a href="/services/">seventeen</a>. Cupola does not come with any.</li>
    <li><strong>Its address and the way it signs in.</strong> Most take an API key: in Radarr, Sonarr, Lidarr, Readarr, Prowlarr and Bazarr it is under <strong>Settings › General</strong>, in Seerr under <strong>Settings › General</strong> too, in Jellyfin under <strong>Dashboard › API Keys</strong>. qBittorrent, Transmission, NZBGet and Dockge take their user and password.</li>
    <li><strong>A way to reach it.</strong> On the same network nothing else is needed. Away from home, use the route you already have: a VPN, or a reverse proxy with <code>https</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="ios">iPhone and iPad</h2>
  {site.downloads.testflight ? (
    <p>Cupola is in beta on TestFlight, Apple's app for testing. Install TestFlight from the App Store, open the <a href={site.downloads.testflight} rel="noopener">invitation link</a> on the phone, and tap <strong>Install</strong>. TestFlight keeps it up to date.</p>
  ) : (
    <p>Cupola will come to iPhone and iPad as a public beta on TestFlight, Apple's app for testing. It is not open yet: <a href={site.watch} rel="noopener">watch releases</a> to hear when it is.</p>
  )}

  <h2 id="android">Android</h2>
  <p>Download the <code>.apk</code> and open it. Android asks once to allow installs from your browser or file manager. A newer release installs over the old one.</p>

  <h2 id="mac">Mac</h2>
  <p>Open the <code>.dmg</code> and drag Cupola to Applications. It is signed with a Developer ID and notarized by Apple, so it opens like any other app.</p>

  <h2 id="desktop">Windows and Linux</h2>
  <p>Every <a href={site.releases} rel="noopener">release</a> includes a Windows and a Linux build. On Windows, unzip it and run <code>cupola.exe</code>; the build is not signed, so SmartScreen asks once. On Linux, untar it and run <code>cupola</code>; it needs GTK 3 and libsecret, present on most desktops. Neither has been tested on a real machine yet, so a report either way helps.</p>

  <h2 id="source">From source</h2>
  <p>Cupola is a Flutter app. With the Flutter SDK named in <code>pubspec.yaml</code>:</p>
  <pre><code>git clone {site.repo}.git
cd {site.repo.split('/').pop()}
flutter pub get
flutter run</code></pre>

  <h2 id="first-run">The first run</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One service at a time.</strong> Cupola asks for what you run and skips the rest. Every connection can be tested before you save it, and says why when it fails.</li>
    <li><strong>Home and away.</strong> A connection can carry a second address for when you are out, and Cupola uses whichever answers.</li>
    <li><strong>A second device in one code.</strong> <strong>Settings › Security › Set up another device</strong> copies every connection to a new phone, encrypted, over your own Wi-Fi.</li>
  </ul>
</Guide>

<style>
  pre {
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding: 18px 20px;
    border-radius: var(--r-button);
    background: rgb(18 18 24 / 0.9);
    border: 1px solid var(--hair);
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  pre code {
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .not-prose {
    max-width: none;
    width: min(var(--well), calc(100vw - 2 * var(--gutter)));
    margin-bottom: 24px;
  }
</style>
